'''Waimakariri''' is a New Zealand parliamentary [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]], formed for the {{NZ election link|1996}} and returning one Member of Parliament to the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]]. The MP for Waimakariri is [[Matt Doocey]] of the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]]. He has held this position since the {{NZ election link|2014}} and takes over from [[Kate Wilkinson (politician)|Kate Wilkinson]], who defeated [[Clayton Cosgrove]] ([[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour]]) in the {{NZ election link|2011}}.

==Population areas==
Waimakariri centres on metropolitan Christchurch and spreads northward up the coast of the South Island. From Christchurch it contains the suburbs of [[Casebrook, New Zealand|Casebrook]] and [[Belfast, New Zealand|Belfast]]; from [[Waimakariri District]] to its north, it takes in the towns of [[Kaiapoi]] and [[Rangiora]] as well as a selection of small inland localities such as [[Cust, New Zealand|Cust]] and [[Oxford, New Zealand|Oxford]]. Boundary changes following the 2006 census were relatively minor; Waimakariri managed to avoid the upheaval wrought upon electorates in Christchurch, losing [[Bishopdale, Christchurch|Bishopdale]] to [[Ilam (New Zealand electorate)|Ilam]] and the last remaining segment of [[Papanui]] to [[Christchurch Central]].

Along with neighbouring [[Selwyn (New Zealand electorate)|Selwyn]], Waimakariri has been experiencing strong population growth, with many people from Christchurch displaced by the earthquakes. In the 2013/14 boundary review by the Representation Commission, Waimakariri lost most of [[Redwood, Canterbury|Redwood]] and Marshland to [[Christchurch Central]] and [[Christchurch East]] respectively, while it gained the less populated [[Harewood, New Zealand|Harewood]] north of Sawyers Arms Road from Selwyn.<ref>{{cite book| title=Report of the Representation Commission 2014 |url= http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |isbn=978-0-477-10414-2 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=26 September 2014 |page=10 |format=PDF |date=4 April 2014}}</ref>

==History==
[[File:Waimakariri electorate 2008.png|right|thumb|The Waimakariri electorate from 2008]]
{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Waimakariri.map|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
The existence of Waimakariri dates back to the introduction of [[Mixed-member proportional representation|MMP]] voting in the [[1996 New Zealand general election|1996 general election]], when the number of South Island electorates fell from twenty-five to sixteen. The electorate originates in the old [[Rangiora (New Zealand electorate)|Rangiora]] electorate, with [[Hurunui District]] shorn off and placed in [[Kaikōura (New Zealand electorate)|Kaikōura]], and the resultant electorate pulled into Christchurch via State Highway 71, absorbing parts of Christchurch previously in the electorate of [[Christchurch North (New Zealand electorate)|Christchurch North]]. The first contest saw Rangiora's [[Jim Gerard]] easily defeated by former Prime Minister and MP for Christchurch North, [[Mike Moore (New Zealand politician)|Mike Moore]].  He left the office in July 1999, having been elected [[Director-General of the World Trade Organization]].

Clayton Cosgrove won the second contest in 1999 and was confirmed in 2002, 2005 and 2008.<ref name="Hon Clayton Cosgrove">{{cite web|title=Hon Clayton Cosgrove|url= http://www.parliament.nz/en-NZ/MPP/MPs/MPs/1/c/d/49MP30571-Cosgrove-Clayton.htm |publisher=New Zealand Parliament |access-date=21 November 2011}}</ref>

Given that Rangiora was a safe National electorate and Christchurch North a safe Labour electorate, and given the urban-rural makeup of the electorate, Waimakariri does not favour any party. At the [[2005 New Zealand general election|2005 election]], while Waimakariri's electors were returning incumbent Clayton Cosgrove by 5,064 votes (and in the process slashing his majority in half), their party vote intentions were more ambiguous, with National winning 79 more party votes than Labour, setting Waimakariri up to be a key electorate at the [[2008 New Zealand general election|2008 election]]. Cosgrove retained the electorate with a much narrower 390 majority in 2008, whilst his opponent [[Kate Wilkinson (politician)|Kate Wilkinson]]'s party (National) got over 5000 more party votes.

Results from the {{NZ election link|2011}} gave Wilkinson a lead of 642 votes over Cosgrove, shifting the electorate from marginal Labour to marginal National.<ref name="2011 election" /> Wilkinson retired at the end of the parliamentary term and was replaced as National's candidate for the {{NZ election link|2014}} by [[Matt Doocey]], who had previously contested the {{By-election link|Christchurch East|2013}} in [[Christchurch East]].<ref name="Wilkinson sword">{{cite news |first=Glenn |last=Conway |newspaper=[[The Press]] |date=8 November 2013 |page=A7 |title=Wilkinson to fall on her sword}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |first=Glenn |last=Conway |newspaper=[[The Press]] |date=8 November 2013 |access-date=9 November 2013 |title=Canterbury MP Kate Wilkinson quits |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/the-press/news/north-canterbury/9372548/Canterbury-MP-Kate-Wilkinson-quits }}</ref> Doocey beat Cosgrove with an increased majority.<ref name="2014 election">{{cite web |title=Official Count Results – Waimakariri |url= http://electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2014/electorate-57.html |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |access-date=27 September 2014 |date=21 September 2014}}</ref>

In the {{NZ election link|2017}}, Doocey beat the Labour candidate, [[Dan Rosewarne]], with an increased majority of that over Cosgrove although the National party vote decreased.<ref name="2017election"/>

